1 NAME David Andersen /Wiborg/
2 GIVN David Andersen
2 SURN Wiborg
2 _MARNM Wiborg
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1673
1 BURI
2 DATE 30 SEP 1752
2 ADDR
3 CITY Arendal
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 SEP 1752
1 OCCU Skrædder
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Skredder.
2 CONT
2 CONT David var gift 3 ganger. Første gang 1.07.1706 med Anne,hun ble begravet 17.12.1706, derpå gift 12.09. 1707 med Johanne Thomasdatter -begravet 30.06. 1740 og tilslutt gift med Ane Olsdatter , som overlevde ham. ( Faye: Bidrag til 
2 CONC Holt prestegjelde ...) og også nevnt i "Arendal geisteliget..." som retteroppnoenfeil)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Arendal Byes Historie - David's hus was the H.P. Nielsen Hus, Matr nr 88a
2 CONT
2 CONT "David Andersen, born 1683 in Viborg, died 1752, first married Anne Christensdatter, and thereafter, after only one year of marriage, married Johanne Thommesdatter, who died 12 years before her husband.  Johan Fürst reports that h
2 CONC e was a tailor, which has however not been confirmed.  On the other hand, the possibility that he was a businessman/trader is more likely, since it is known/recorded that a firm in Scotland made four bills of exchange for him in t
2 CONC he year 1735, and that because of this debt, Andersen pawned his house and everything that he owned both "at home and abroad" [presumably quoting from the original document]. This indicates that he was involved in shipping and him
2 CONC self imported goods from Scotland.  That a tailor at this time could have direct connections with Scottish producers is unlikely.  He later moved to Tyholmen.  On a record dated the 25th of April 1730, which can be found both in t
2 CONC he town's oldest pawnbook and which Johan Fürst kept in the original, DavidAndersen sells the house to the previously named Christen Christensen Kastrup.  From the document it is seen that he paid a basic fee for the plot.  It was
2 CONC  witnessed by its owner Karen sal Christen Thommesen ["sal"is presumably an old-fashioned form of address, or a title], Søfren Andersen's widow; she approved the sale of the house.  The land with the pier and wavebreakers at Polle
2 CONC n [a harbor area in Arendal] had been signed over by her already in 1709.  But the house was for sale only partially; it is said that the main house and the hall were finished, but the hearth was missing."
2 CONT
2 CONT Also two birth records (from parish records) for Jens Davidsen & Peter Davidsen (1720), parents listed as David Anderssen Schreder & JohanneThomasedtr.
